Standard uncertainty, expanded uncertainty, and coverage claims (@met-coverage: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @metrology, #source "../sources/GUM-and-Amendment.lingua#r_3KA76CK3QAA3M470TC3XHP19MQ", #source "../../quantitative-literacy/sources/Uncertainty-Guides.lingua#r_11PDFVNTH65FKFVZVHMHMAF6CH", #source "../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E", #metrology "Nonlinearity-and-bias.lingua#r_7RZPX9400CJWKT2SH1S8WGFWMW") { r_5Q2X2FWBE479BM2WERZQNG9PCV
**Read:** [GUM — JCGM 100:2008 and the 2026 nonlinearity amendment](../sources/GUM-and-Amendment.lingua#r_3KA76CK3QAA3M470TC3XHP19MQ), expanded uncertainty, reporting, and coverage annexes; [Uncertainty guides — Taylor, Kuyatt, and JCGM](../../quantitative-literacy/sources/Uncertainty-Guides.lingua#r_11PDFVNTH65FKFVZVHMHMAF6CH), sections 6–7 and coverage guidance; [Simple Guide for Evaluating and Expressing Uncertainty — Possolo](../sources/Uncertainty-NIST-1900.lingua#r_75WCVW8CJG4TAFD3330T87588E), section 8 and interval examples.

A standard uncertainty summarizes dispersion in standard-deviation form. An expanded uncertainty U = ku_c uses a coverage factor under a stated interpretation. A coverage interval or region needs a probability statement and the model supporting it. A confidence interval from a repeated-sampling procedure and a probabilistic state-of-knowledge interval are not interchangeable merely because their endpoints look similar.

For a normal output distribution with sufficiently well-known parameters, an interval of ±1.96 standard deviations has approximately 95% probability. Using k = 2 can be a convenient approximation in suitable conditions; it is not a universal 95% rule for skewed outputs, small effective degrees of freedom, or invalid input assumptions.

For independent contributions under the applicable GUM approximation, effective degrees of freedom can be estimated as ν_eff = u_c⁴/Σ[(c_i u_i)⁴/ν_i]. Read the assumptions before applying a Student-t coverage factor. Infinite degrees of freedom for a contribution is an evaluation assumption, not a reward for calling it Type B.

**Work:** compare an independent normal-mean example using a known standard deviation with one estimating it from a small sample. State the corresponding interval interpretation and degrees of freedom. Then examine a bounded or strongly skewed output where a symmetric expanded interval is a poor summary. Explain why a shortest interval and an equal-tail interval can both have the same coverage but different endpoints.

Report k, coverage probability when justified, method, relevant distribution assumptions, and enough information to reconstruct the result. Keep the unrounded calculation while presenting a sensible number of digits. A recipient cannot interpret “±0.1” reliably without knowing whether it is a tolerance, bound, standard uncertainty, or expanded uncertainty.

**Continue:** [Nonlinearity can change the estimated value as well as its uncertainty](Nonlinearity-and-bias.lingua#r_7RZPX9400CJWKT2SH1S8WGFWMW).

**Return:** [Measurement and metrology](../Metrology.lingua#r_3G54ENPA974K19AB5XF0P641N5).
